    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 263 tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented a derived `timezone` field on the owner response, mapped from the owner's locality/region via the fixed table (NSW\u2192Australia/Sydney, VIC\u2192Australia/Melbourne, QLD\u2192Australia/Brisbane).\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `timezone` string property to the Owner schema (right after `locality`), documenting the region-to-timezone table. This is what generates the `timezone` field on `OwnerDto`.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerLocality.java`** \u2014 Added a fixed `REGION_TIMEZONE` table and a `timezoneFromCustomerCode(String)` helper that resolves the region from the owner's `customerCode` (the same source as `locality`) and looks up its IANA timezone, returning `null` for unknown regions so the field is simply omitted.\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added a `@Mapping` for `timezone` that calls the new helper, mirroring how `locality` is derived.\n\nThe design keeps `timezone` consistent with `locality` \u2014 both derive from the region component of the `customerCode` \u2014 and follows the existing conventions in this file (helpers kept out of the `@Mapper` interface so MapStruct doesn't treat them as implicit `String \u2192 String` mappings).\n\nVerification: the new `AcceptanceTest.coreReturnsTimezoneForRegion` passes (Sydney/postcode 2000 \u2192 locality `NSW`, timezone `Australia/Sydney`), and the full suite is green \u2014 **263 tests, 0 failures**.",
